STIFF-MAN SYNDROME : A CASE REPORT
AND REVIEW OF THE LITERATURE

Aziz Sonawalla*

Stiff-man syndrome is a rare disorder of
motor function, characterised by involuntary
stiffness of axial muscles and superimposed
painful muscles spasms. Being a rare disorder, it can easily be confused with other disease processes, and the diagnosis can be
missed for long periods of time. A careful
assessment and clinical work up, with a high
index of suspicion is therefore essential.

The following is perhaps the first case
report of stiff-man syndrome from Pakistan.

<seg id="s0001" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">CASE REPORT:</seg>

<seg id="s0002" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">MAQ, a 50-year-old male, was first seen
in the outpatient clinic at Aga Khan University Hospital in 1988, complaining of low
back pain radiating to the thighs, persistent
for the last four years. The pain was gradually
progressive, increasing on exertion and bending. There was no particular dermatomal
distribution of pain. X-rays of the lumbosacral spine were negative and neurological
examinations were usually reported as unremarkable. He was advised to take regular</seg>

